#sec("I", "Problem Formulation and Mathematical Model")

Let $X = \{x_1, dots, x_n\}$ be a finite set of $n >= 2$ objects with symmetric, zero-diagonal dissimilarity matrix $D = (d_(i j)) in bb(R)_(>= 0)^(n times n)$. Whereas agglomerative algorithms (Algs. 58--62) build hierarchies *bottom-up* by merging $n - 1$ pairs starting from singletons, *divisive* hierarchical clustering operates *top-down*: it initializes the hierarchy with the single root cluster $cal(C)^((0)) = \{X\}$ and recursively bipartitions one selected cluster at each step $t = 1, dots, n - 1$ until all clusters are singletons (or until a desired target count $k$ is reached).

An exact exhaustive bipartition of a cluster $C$ of size $m$ must search $2^(m-1) - 1$ nontrivial cuts, which is intractable beyond $m approx 25$. Building on the splinter-group heuristic of MacNaughton-Smith et al. (1964), Kaufman and Rousseeuw @kaufman1990finding formalized *DIANA* (*Divisive Analysis*) as a polynomial-time, purely dissimilarity-based top-down algorithm. Because DIANA examines global pairwise dissimilarities over the entire dataset at step $1$, its top-level splits are informed by macro-scale cluster geometry rather than local pairwise merges that can accumulate irreversible errors bottom-up.

#definition(title: "DIANA Diameter Selection and Splinter Separation Criterion")[
  For any non-empty subset $C subset.eq X$ with $|C| >= 2$, define its *diameter* and the *mean dissimilarity* from an element $x in C$ to a disjoint or remaining set $U subset.eq X$ ($U != emptyset$) by
  $ "diam"(C) = max_(u, v in C) d(u, v), quad bar(d)(x, U) = 1 / |U| sum_(y in U) d(x, y). $
  At split step $t in \{1, dots, n - 1\}$, DIANA chooses a cluster of maximum diameter:
  $ C_t^* in arg max_(C in cal(C)^((t-1)), \, |C| >= 2) "diam"(C), quad h_t = "diam"(C_t^*). $
  If $|C_t^*| >= 3$, DIANA initializes a *splinter group* $S = \{x^*\}$ using the most globally dissimilar seed
  $ x^* in arg max_(x in C_t^*) bar(d)(x, C_t^* without \{x\}), $
  sets the remainder $R = C_t^* without \{x^*\}$, and defines for each $x in R$ (while $|R| >= 2$) the *net separation score*
  $ Delta(x; R, S) = bar(d)(x, R without \{x\}) - bar(d)(x, S). $
  Elements migrate greedily and irreversibly from $R$ to $S$ as long as $max_(x in R) Delta(x; R, S) > 0$.
]

By maintaining incremental per-object dissimilarity sums $A_R (x) = sum_(y in R without \{x\}) d(x, y)$ and $A_S (x) = sum_(z in S) d(x, z)$, each split of a cluster of size $m$ executes in $Theta(m^2)$ worst-case time rather than naive $cal(O)(m^3)$ recomputation.

#algbox(
  title: "DIANA (Divisive Analysis) with Incremental Sum Updates",
  inputs: [Symmetric dissimilarity matrix $D in bb(R)_(>= 0)^(n times n)$; optional target $k in \{2, dots, n\}$ (default $k = n$).],
  outputs: [Divisive split hierarchy $\{(C_t^*, R_t, S_t, h_t)\}_(t=1)^(k-1)$.],
)[
  1. Initialize $cal(C) <- \{X\}$ and compute $"diam"(X) = max_(i, j) d_(i j)$.
  2. For $t = 1, dots, k - 1$:
     a. Select $C^* in arg max_(C in cal(C), \, |C| >= 2) "diam"(C)$ and record split height $h_t <- "diam"(C^*)$.
     b. If $|C^*| = 2$, set $R <- \{u\}$, $S <- \{v\}$ for $C^* = \{u, v\}$ and go to Step 2(f).
     c. For every $x in C^*$, compute $T(x) <- sum_(y in C^* without \{x\}) d(x, y)$; pick seed $x^* in arg max_(x in C^*) T(x)$.
     d. Set $S <- \{x^*\}$, $R <- C^* without \{x^*\}$; for every $x in R$, initialize $A_S (x) <- d(x, x^*)$ and $A_R (x) <- T(x) - d(x, x^*)$.
     e. While $|R| >= 2$:
        - Compute $Delta(x) <- A_R (x) / (|R| - 1) - A_S (x) / |S|$ for all $x in R$, and pick $x^+ in arg max_(x in R) Delta(x)$.
        - If $Delta(x^+) <= 0$, break.
        - Move $x^+$: $S <- S union \{x^+\}$, $R <- R without \{x^+\}$; for each remaining $x in R$, update $A_R (x) <- A_R (x) - d(x, x^+)$ and $A_S (x) <- A_S (x) + d(x, x^+)$.
     f. Compute $"diam"(R)$ and $"diam"(S)$, update $cal(C) <- (cal(C) without \{C^*\}) union \{R, S\}$, and record $(C^*, R, S, h_t)$.
  3. Return the divisive hierarchy.
]

#sec("II", "Formal Mathematical Proofs and Theoretical Guarantees")

#subsec("A", "Monotone Diameter Decay and Dominating Ultrametricity")

Because DIANA labels each split by the diameter of the split cluster, its hierarchy is guaranteed to be inversion-free and always generates a valid *dominating* ultrametric.

#theorem(title: "Monotone Split Heights and Dominating Cophenetic Ultrametric", origin: "Original Proof")[
  Let $h_t = "diam"(C_t^*)$ ($t = 1, dots, n - 1$) be the split heights generated by a full run of DIANA on any symmetric dissimilarity matrix $D in bb(R)_(>= 0)^(n times n)$ with $d_(i i) = 0$. Define the cophenetic dissimilarity $u_"DIANA" (x_i, x_j) = h_(t(i, j))$ for $i != j$ (and $u_"DIANA" (x_i, x_i) = 0$), where $t(i, j)$ is the unique split step separating $x_i$ from $x_j$. Then:
  1. *Inversion-free monotonicity*: $"diam"(X) = h_1 >= h_2 >= dots >= h_(n-1) >= 0$.
  2. *Dominating ultrametric*: $u_"DIANA"$ is an ultrametric on $X$ satisfying
     $ u_"DIANA" (x, z) <= max(u_"DIANA" (x, y), u_"DIANA" (y, z)) quad forall x, y, z in X, $
     and $u_"DIANA" (x_i, x_j) >= d(x_i, x_j)$ for all $x_i, x_j in X$.
]

#proof[
  For (1), at step $t$, $C_t^*$ is partitioned into proper non-empty subsets $R_t, S_t subset C_t^*$. Monotonicity of the maximum over subsets yields $"diam"(R_t) <= "diam"(C_t^*) = h_t$ and $"diam"(S_t) <= "diam"(C_t^*) = h_t$. Every other cluster $C in cal(C)^((t-1)) without \{C_t^*\}$ already obeyed $"diam"(C) <= h_t$ by optimality of $C_t^*$. Hence every active cluster in $cal(C)^((t))$ has diameter at most $h_t$, implying $h_(t+1) = max_(C in cal(C)^((t)), |C| >= 2) "diam"(C) <= h_t$.

  For (2), symmetry and non-negativity are immediate. Fix any three distinct objects $x, y, z in X$, and let $m = min(t(x, y), t(y, z))$. Immediately prior to step $m$, $x$ and $y$ still belong to the same active cluster, and $y$ and $z$ still belong to the same active cluster. By transitivity of equivalence classes, $x$ and $z$ are still unseparated prior to step $m$, so $t(x, z) >= m$. Applying the non-increasing property (1) gives
  $ u_"DIANA" (x, z) = h_(t(x, z)) <= h_m = max(h_(t(x, y)), h_(t(y, z))) \ = max(u_"DIANA" (x, y), u_"DIANA" (y, z)), $
  establishing the strong triangle inequality. Finally, since $x_i, x_j in C_(t(i, j))^*$, we have $d(x_i, x_j) <= max_(u, v in C_(t(i, j))^*) d(u, v) = h_(t(i, j)) = u_"DIANA" (x_i, x_j)$.
]

#subsec("B", "Exact Recovery Threshold and Non-Recovery of Unbalanced Ultrametrics")

Every classical agglomerative linkage without inversions (Single, Complete, UPGMA, WPGMA) recovers *every* exact ultrametric $u^*$ identically. Remarkably, we prove that DIANA does *not* share universal ultrametric recovery: exact top-down recovery requires a quantitative balance-to-separation ratio, and fails on an explicit $n = 6$ strict ultrametric!

#theorem(title: "Sufficient Separation-Imbalance Recovery Bound and n = 6 Ultrametric Counterexample", origin: "Original Counterexample")[
  Let $u^*$ be an ultrametric on $X = A union B$ ($A inter B = emptyset$) with top clade height $H = u^*(A, B)$ and internal sub-clade bound $h_"max" = max("diam"(A), "diam"(B)) < H$.
  1. *Sufficient exact recovery condition*: If either $min(|A|, |B|) = 1$, or $|A|, |B| >= 2$ and
     $ H / h_"max" > 1 + (max(|A|, |B|) - 2) / min(|A|, |B|), $
     then DIANA's first split recovers the exact top-level clade partition $\{A, B\}$ with height $h_1 = H$.
  2. *Failure of universal ultrametric recovery*: There exists a strict $6$-point ultrametric $u^*$ on which DIANA produces a top-level split incompatible with the clades of $u^*$ and strictly overestimates $u^*$.
]

#proof[
  For (1), first suppose $min(|A|, |B|) = 1$, say $B = \{b\}$ and $|A| = n - 1 >= 1$. Then $bar(d)(b, X without \{b\}) = H$, while every $x in A$ has $bar(d)(x, X without \{x\}) <= (H + (n - 2) h_"max") / (n - 1) < H$ (for $n >= 3$; for $n = 2$ the split is trivial). Thus DIANA uniquely seeds $S = \{b\}$, $R = A$. Every $x in A$ then has $bar(d)(x, S) = H$ and $bar(d)(x, A without \{x\}) <= h_"max" < H$, giving $Delta(x; R, S) < 0$ and immediate termination with $\{A, B\}$.

  Now assume $|A|, |B| >= 2$ and without loss of generality that the initial seed satisfies $x^* in B$. We prove by induction on $s = |S| in \{1, dots, |B| - 1\}$ that $S subset B$, $Delta(x; R, S) < 0$ for every $x in A$, and $Delta(y; R, S) > 0$ for every $y in B without S$. Write $R = A union B'$ with $B' = B without S$ and $|B'| = |B| - s >= 1$.
  - For any $x in A$, $bar(d)(x, S) = H$, while
    $ bar(d)(x, R without \{x\}) <= (|B'| H + (|A| - 1) h_"max") / (|A| + |B'| - 1) < H, $
    since $|A| >= 2$ and $h_"max" < H$. Hence $Delta(x; R, S) < 0$ for all $x in A$: no element of $A$ can ever enter $S$.
  - For any $y in B'$, $bar(d)(y, S) <= h_"max"$, whereas $u^* >= 0$ and $s >= 1$ yield
    $ bar(d)(y, R without \{y\}) >= (|A| H) / (|A| + |B| - s - 1) >= (|A| H) / (|A| + |B| - 2). $
    By hypothesis, $H / h_"max" > 1 + (|B| - 2) / |A| = (|A| + |B| - 2) / |A|$, so $(|A| H) / (|A| + |B| - 2) > h_"max"$, ensuring $Delta(y; R, S) > 0$ for every $y in B'$.
  Consequently, DIANA transfers elements exclusively from $B'$ into $S$ until $S = B$ and $R = A$. At that stage every $x in A$ satisfies $Delta(x; A, B) <= h_"max" - H < 0$, terminating with exact split $\{A, B\}$.

  For (2), construct the $n = 6$ strict ultrametric tree $T^* = ((a_1, a_2)_1, (b_1, (c_1, c_2, c_3)_1)_9)_(10)$, where $A = \{a_1, a_2\}$, $B = \{b_1, c_1, c_2, c_3\}$, $u^*(A, B) = 10$, $u^*(b_1, c_j) = 9$, and $u^*(a_1, a_2) = u^*(c_i, c_j) = 1$ ($i != j$). All pairwise distances are strictly positive and satisfy the ultrametric inequality ($1 < 9 < 10$). Compute seed distance sums (over $n - 1 = 5$ neighbors):
  - $a_1, a_2$: $1 + 4(10) = 41$ ($bar(d) = 8.2$);
  - $b_1$: $2(10) + 3(9) = 47$ ($bar(d) = 9.4$, unique maximum);
  - $c_1, c_2, c_3$: $2(10) + 9 + 2(1) = 31$ ($bar(d) = 6.2$).
  DIANA uniquely seeds $S = \{b_1\}$ and $R = \{a_1, a_2, c_1, c_2, c_3\}$ ($|R| - 1 = 4$). Evaluating $Delta(x; R, S)$:
  $ Delta(a_i; R, S) &= (1 + 3(10)) / 4 - 10 = 7.75 - 10 = -2.25 < 0, \ Delta(c_j; R, S) &= (2(10) + 2(1)) / 4 - 9 = 5.50 - 9 = -3.50 < 0. $
  Because $max_(x in R) Delta(x; R, S) = -2.25 <= 0$, DIANA terminates Step 1 immediately with $\{b_1\} | \{a_1, a_2, c_1, c_2, c_3\}$, which violates the unique top clade split $\{a_1, a_2\} | \{b_1, c_1, c_2, c_3\}$ and assigns $u_"DIANA" (b_1, c_1) = 10 > 9 = u^*(b_1, c_1)$.
]

#subsec("C", "Exact Silhouette Equivalence and Cut-Ascent Regime")

Although DIANA's splintering rule is often described purely heuristically, it admits an exact equivalence with Rousseeuw's Silhouette width @rousseeuw1987silhouettes and a strict Lyapunov cut-ascent property during the first half of any split.

#proposition(title: "Silhouette-Sign Equivalence, Cut-Ascent Regime, and Finite Termination", origin: "Original Derivation")[
  During any split of $C^*$ ($|C^*| = m >= 3$) into remainder $R$ ($|R| = r >= 2$) and splinter $S$ ($|S| = s >= 1$), let $s_"sil" (x; R, S)$ denote the Silhouette width of $x in R$ in the bipartition $\{R, S\}$, $E(R, S) = sum_(u in R, v in S) d(u, v)$ the cut weight, and $W(R) = sum_(\{u, v\} subset R) d(u, v)$ the internal dissimilarity sum. Then:
  1. *Silhouette equivalence*: For every $x in R$,
     $ s_"sil" (x; R, S) = (-Delta(x; R, S)) / max(bar(d)(x, R without \{x\}), bar(d)(x, S)), $
     so $Delta(x; R, S) > 0 <=> s_"sil" (x; R, S) < 0$. Upon loop termination, *every* object in $R$ satisfies $s_"sil" (x; R, S) >= 0$.
  2. *Strict cut ascent / dispersion descent*: Whenever $s <= r - 1$ (every transfer before $S$ becomes a strict majority), moving $x^+$ with $Delta(x^+; R, S) > 0$ strictly increases $E(R, S)$ and strictly decreases total within-cluster dispersion $W(R) + W(S)$ by $A_R (x^+) - A_S (x^+) > 0$.
  3. *Finite termination*: Unidirectional transfer terminates in at most $m - 2$ iterations without cycles.
]

#proof[
  (1) By definition @rousseeuw1987silhouettes, for $x in R$ in the 2-cluster partition $\{R, S\}$, $a(x) = bar(d)(x, R without \{x\})$ and $b(x) = bar(d)(x, S)$, so $s_"sil" (x; R, S) = (b(x) - a(x)) / max(a(x), b(x)) = -Delta(x; R, S) / max(a(x), b(x))$. Since the denominator is strictly positive whenever $Delta != 0$, $Delta > 0$ iff $s_"sil" < 0$. Termination occurs iff $Delta(x; R, S) <= 0$ for all $x in R$, meaning $s_"sil" (x; R, S) >= 0$ for all $x in R$.

  (2) Writing $Delta(x^+; R, S) = A_R (x^+) / (r - 1) - A_S (x^+) / s > 0$ gives $A_R (x^+) > ((r - 1) / s) A_S (x^+)$. Whenever $s <= r - 1$, $(r - 1) / s >= 1$ and $A_S (x^+) >= 0$, so $A_R (x^+) - A_S (x^+) > 0$. Under $R' = R without \{x^+\}$ and $S' = S union \{x^+\}$, every pair between $x^+$ and $R'$ enters the cut while every pair between $x^+$ and $S$ leaves the cut, so $E(R', S') - E(R, S) = A_R (x^+) - A_S (x^+) > 0$. Since $W(R) + W(S) + E(R, S) = W(C^*)$ is constant, $W(R') + W(S')$ drops by the same positive amount.

  (3) Every iteration reduces $|R|$ by $1$ starting from $m - 1$ and stops once $|R| = 1$, bounding iterations by $m - 2$.
]

#subsec("D", "Exact Incremental Complexity Across Balanced, Unbalanced, and Top-k Regimes")

#proposition(title: "Tight Balanced, Worst-Case, and Truncated Top-k Complexity Bounds", origin: "Original Proof")[
  With incremental sum updates (Algorithm box):
  1. Splitting a cluster of size $m$ runs in $Theta(m^2)$ worst-case time (improving naive $cal(O)(m^3)$ recomputation).
  2. Computing the full $n$-leaf hierarchy takes $Theta(n^2)$ time under constant-factor balanced splits ($max(|R|, |S|) <= rho m$ for fixed $rho in [1/2, 1)$) and $Theta(n^3)$ worst-case time under maximally unbalanced splits ($|S| = 1$ at every step).
  3. Computing a flat top-$k$ partition ($k << n$) takes $cal(O)(k n^2)$ worst-case time and $cal(O)(n^2)$ space.
]

#proof[
  (1) Computing initial sums $T(x)$ for $m$ objects requires $m(m - 1)$ additions; each of the at most $m - 2$ transfers scans $|R| <= m$ elements to find $x^+$ and performs $2(|R| - 1)$ constant-time updates to $A_R, A_S$, totaling at most $c m^2$ operations for a universal constant $c > 0$. Computing $"diam"(R)$ and $"diam"(S)$ adds at most $binom(m, 2) = cal(O)(m^2)$ comparisons.

  (2) Under balanced splits $|R| = alpha m$, $|S| = (1 - alpha) m$ with $1 - rho <= alpha <= rho$, convexity of $alpha |-> alpha^2 + (1 - alpha)^2$ gives $alpha^2 + (1 - alpha)^2 <= rho^2 + (1 - rho)^2 =: gamma < 1$. By induction, $T(m) <= (c / (1 - gamma)) m^2$:
  $ T(m) &<= c m^2 + c / (1 - gamma) (alpha^2 + (1 - alpha)^2) m^2 \ &<= c m^2 (1 + gamma / (1 - gamma)) = c / (1 - gamma) m^2 = Theta(m^2). $
  For unbalanced splits ($m -> (m - 1, 1)$), $T(n) = sum_(m=2)^n c m^2 = c (n (n + 1) (2 n + 1) / 6 - 1) = Theta(n^3)$.

  (3) Truncating at $k$ clusters invokes at most $k - 1$ splits of cluster sizes $m_1, dots, m_(k-1) <= n$, giving $sum_(j=1)^(k-1) cal(O)(m_j^2) = cal(O)(k n^2)$ time.
]

#subsec("E", "Transformation Equivariance and Non-Invariance to Monotone Warping")

#proposition(title: "Positive Affine Equivariance and Constructive Non-Invariance to Monotone Warping", origin: "Original Counterexample")[
  1. Under any positive affine transformation $tilde(d)(x, y) = a d(x, y) + b$ ($a > 0$, $b >= 0$, $x != y$), DIANA produces the exact same divisive tree with heights $tilde(h)_t = a h_t + b$.
  2. DIANA is not invariant under strictly increasing nonlinear transforms $phi(d)$: on $X = \{0, 1, 20, 22, 38\} subset bb(R)$, $d(x, y) = |x - y|$ and $phi(d) = d^2$ produce distinct top-level splits.
]

#proof[
  (1) For every cluster $C$ ($|C| >= 2$), $"diam"_(tilde(d)) (C) = a "diam"_d (C) + b$ and $bar(tilde(d))(x, C without \{x\}) = a bar(d)(x, C without \{x\}) + b$, preserving both cluster and seed selection. Furthermore, $tilde(Delta)(x; R, S) = (a bar(d)(x, R without \{x\}) + b) - (a bar(d)(x, S) + b) = a Delta(x; R, S)$, preserving sign and maximizer identically.

  (2) Trace Step 1 on $X = \{0, 1, 20, 22, 38\}$:
  - Under $d(x, y) = |x - y|$, distance sums are $T(0) = 81$, $T(1) = 78$, $T(20) = 59$, $T(22) = 61$, and $T(38) = 109$ (unique seed $x^* = 38$). With $S = \{38\}$ and $R = \{0, 1, 20, 22\}$,
    $ Delta(22) = (22 + 21 + 2) / 3 - 16 = 15 - 16 = -1 < 0, $
    and $Delta(20) = 41/3 - 18 < 0$, $Delta(1) < 0$, $Delta(0) < 0$. DIANA stops immediately with $\{\{38\}, \{0, 1, 20, 22\}\}$.
  - Under $phi(r) = r^2$, distance sums are $T_phi (0) = 2329$, $T_phi (1) = 2172$, $T_phi (20) = 1089$, $T_phi (22) = 1185$, and $T_phi (38) = 3393$ (still seed $x^* = 38$). Now
    $ Delta_phi (22) = (484 + 441 + 4) / 3 - 256 = 929 / 3 - 256 approx +53.67 > 0, $
    moving $22$ into $S = \{22, 38\}$. Next, $Delta_phi (20) = (400 + 361)/2 - (4 + 324)/2 = +216.5 > 0$ moves $20$ into $S = \{20, 22, 38\}$, after which $Delta_phi (0), Delta_phi (1) < 0$, yielding the strictly distinct split $\{\{0, 1\}, \{20, 22, 38\}\}$.
]

#sec("III", "Complexity, Geometry and Operational Profile")

DIANA's operational profile contrasts sharply with bottom-up agglomerative schemes. For constructing a *complete* $n$-leaf dendrogram, DIANA's worst-case complexity is $Theta(n^3)$ (triggered by skewed outlier-peeling sequences, Proposition 2(2)), slower than $cal(O)(n^2)$ NN-chain WPGMA (Alg. 60). However, in practical data analysis where practitioners seek a *top-$k$ partition* ($k << n$, e.g., $k in [2, 20]$), DIANA only performs $k - 1$ splits and stops early in $cal(O)(k n^2)$ time — bypassing the $n - k$ fine-grained bottom-up merges that every agglomerative linkage must compute before reaching the top $k$ clusters! Memory usage is $cal(O)(n^2)$ for the dissimilarity matrix plus $cal(O)(n)$ for incremental sum arrays $A_R, A_S$, exhibiting sequential row-wise cache reads during each inner update loop.

Geometrically, DIANA combines a *max-diameter* macro-scheduling criterion (which forces wide clusters to subdivide before compact ones, mimicking complete linkage's tendency to bound cluster diameters) with an *average-dissimilarity* bipartition rule (whose stopping condition enforces non-negative Silhouette width for every element in $R$, Proposition 1(1)). Because the top split aggregates distances across all $n - 1$ peers simultaneously, DIANA averages out local nearest-neighbor noise that plagues bottom-up methods in high dimensions @beyer1999nearest, while remaining sensitive to single extreme outliers that inflate cluster diameter and monopolize the initial seed $x^*$.

